\begin_layout Subsection
Propagation Mechanism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The propagation mechanism facilitates the exchange of information among subpopulations by periodically sharing high-quality candidate solutions throughout the optimization process.
 During each propagation stage,
 the best solution identified within a selected subpopulation is transferred to one or more target subpopulations,
 where it replaces the poorest-performing individual.
 By continuously introducing promising solutions across subpopulations,
 this mechanism strengthens cooperation,
 promotes knowledge sharing and guides the search toward more promising regions of the solution space.
 To ensure low communication overhead while preserving scalability,
 the proposed framework employs lightweight propagation schemes rather than conventional migration policies.
 The following propagation strategies are investigated:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One-to-One (1to1):
 A randomly chosen subpopulation transfers its current best solution to another randomly selected subpopulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One-to-All (1toN):
 The best solution found by a randomly selected subpopulation is distributed to every remaining subpopulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
All-to-One (Nto1):
 The best solutions produced by all subpopulations are transmitted to a single randomly selected recipient subpopulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
All-to-All (NtoN):
 Every subpopulation shares its best solution with each of the remaining subpopulations,
 enabling comprehensive information exchange.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Collectively,
 these propagation strategies facilitate the dissemination of high-quality candidate solutions while maintaining the computational efficiency and scalability of the proposed parallel optimization framework.
\end_layout

