\begin_layout Standard
The benchmark suite was intentionally selected to cover a broad spectrum of optimization challenges.
 Unimodal functions mainly evaluate convergence speed and exploitation capability,
 whereas multimodal functions assess the ability of the optimizer to avoid premature convergence and escape local optima.
 Ill-conditioned functions examine numerical robustness under narrow search valleys,
 while composite and weak global structure functions evaluate the balance between exploration and exploitation.
 Finally,
 non-separable problems assess the capability of the algorithm to optimize strongly interacting decision variables,
 which are frequently encountered in real-world optimization problems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Experimental Environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All benchmark functions described in the previous subsection were evaluated under the same experimental conditions.
 The experiments were performed on a workstation equipped with an AMD RYZEN 5950X processor and 128 GB of RAM,
 running the Debian Linux operating system.
 To ensure statistical reliability,
 each benchmark problem was independently executed 30 times,
 using a different random seed for every run,
 and the reported results correspond to the average performance over these independent executions.
 The proposed algorithm was implemented in C++ within the publicly available OPTIMUS optimization framework 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "OPTIMUS"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 which can be downloaded from 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
https://github.com/itsoulos/OPTIMUS
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (accessed on 23 June 2026).
 Employing an open-source optimization framework enhances the reproducibility of the experimental study and enables independent verification of the reported results by other researchers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Parameter Setting and Evaluation Protocol
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All optimization algorithms included in the comparative study were implemented independently within the same experimental framework rather than directly using existing implementations from the literature.
 To ensure a fair and consistent comparison,
 every algorithm was developed in C++ and executed under identical hardware and software conditions.
 In addition,
 the same stopping criterion,
 population size,
 maximum number of iterations,
 and evaluation protocol were adopted for all methods,
 while identical parameter values were used whenever applicable.
 For all algorithms,
 the population size was fixed at 200 individuals,
 the maximum number of iterations was set to 200 and the local search probability was maintained at 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}=0.05$
\end_inset

.
 Using a common experimental configuration ensures that the observed performance differences primarily reflect the characteristics of the optimization algorithms rather than differences in the evaluation environment.
 Algorithm-specific parameters were selected according to the recommendations reported in the corresponding publications and the original EAO algorithm,
 and these values were kept unchanged throughout all benchmark problems without problem-dependent parameter tuning.
 Although a dedicated sensitivity analysis of all parameters is beyond the scope of this work,
 the proposed method demonstrated stable and consistent performance under the selected configuration.
