\begin_layout Standard
At iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $iter$
\end_inset

,
 the convergence-based stopping criterion computes the absolute difference between the best objective-function values obtained in two consecutive iterations:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{(iter)}=\left|f^{(iter)}_{\min}-f^{(iter-1)}_{\min}\right|.$
\end_inset

 A run terminates when 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{(iter)}\le\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{I}=8$
\end_inset

 consecutive iterations or when the maximum number of iterations is reached.
 In all experiments,
 the tolerance was set to 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon=10^{-6}$
\end_inset

.
 Termination indicates that no meaningful improvement has been observed according to the adopted stopping criterion and does not necessarily imply that the global optimum has been identified.
 The reported number of objective function evaluations corresponds to the total number of objective function calls performed from initialization until the stopping criterion is satisfied.
 Objective function evaluations incurred during candidate generation and local search refinement were also included in the reported evaluation count.
 This evaluation protocol is consistent with common practice in benchmarking evolutionary optimization algorithms,
 where all objective function evaluations contribute to the computational effort.
 For benchmark functions with a known global optimum 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{*},$
\end_inset

 the same numerical tolerance was adopted to determine whether a run was successful.
 Specifically,
 a run was considered successful if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|f_{\min}-f^{*}\right|\le\varepsilon.$
\end_inset

 Runs that terminated without satisfying this condition were not discarded instead,
 their final objective values and the corresponding numbers of objective function evaluations were included in the reported statistics.
 Consequently,
 the reported evaluation counts represent the computational effort consumed until termination rather than the number of evaluations required to reach the global optimum.
 Values reported in parentheses indicate the success rate,
 expressed as the proportion of successful runs among the 30 independent executions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sensitivity Analysis of PEAO
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Effect of the Number of Subpopulations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:3.3 Impact of the Number of Clusters"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 compares the average number of objective function evaluations obtained using different numbers of subpopulations (5,
 10,
 20 and 30).
 Lower values indicate better computational efficiency.
 Overall,
 the number of subpopulations has a substantial impact on the performance of the proposed PEAO algorithm.
 The configurations with 10 and 20 subpopulations consistently achieve the lowest computational effort across the considered benchmark problems,
 whereas 5 and 30 subpopulations generally require a larger number of objective function evaluations.
 The cumulative results further support this observation,
 with the total number of objective function evaluations decreasing from 385,708 for 5 subpopulations to 297,299 and 286,096 for 10 and 20 subpopulations,
 respectively,
 before increasing to 351,709 for 30 subpopulations.
 These findings indicate that a moderate number of subpopulations provides the best balance between exploration and exploitation,
 resulting in improved convergence and computational efficiency under the examined experimental configuration.
\end_layout

