\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Impact-of-the"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents the influence of the number of clusters on the computational efficiency of the proposed algorithm.
 The results reveal that the configurations employing 10 and 20 clusters require significantly fewer function evaluations than the alternatives while also demonstrating lower variability across independent runs.
 Conversely,
 the 5-cluster configuration exhibits the highest median number of function calls and substantial dispersion,
 indicating slower and less reliable convergence.
 Although the 30-cluster configuration performs better than the 5-cluster case,
 its variability remains considerably higher than that observed for 10 and 20 clusters.
 The Kruskal–Wallis test (
\begin_inset Formula $p=8.4×10⁻^{¹²}$
\end_inset

) indicates statistically significant differences among the examined configurations.
 These findings suggest that using a moderate number of clusters can substantially enhance the search efficiency and robustness of the algorithm,
 with 10 and 20 clusters yielding the most favorable performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Effect of the Propagation Mechanism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:3.4 Impact of Propagation Mechanism"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 compares the proposed PEAO algorithm with and without the propagation mechanism (PROP = 1 and PROP = 0) in terms of the average number of objective function evaluations.
 Lower values indicate better computational efficiency.
 Overall,
 enabling the propagation mechanism consistently reduces the number of objective function evaluations across all benchmark problems,
 with the most pronounced improvements observed for the higher-dimensional benchmark instances.
 The cumulative results further demonstrate the effectiveness of propagation,
 reducing the total number of objective function evaluations from 1,897,933 without propagation to 297,299 with propagation,
 corresponding to an overall reduction of approximately 84%.
 These results indicate that the propagation mechanism substantially enhances information sharing among subpopulations,
 accelerates convergence,
 and significantly improves the computational efficiency of the proposed framework.
\end_layout

