\begin_layout Subsubsection
Effect of Propagation Methods with 10 Subpopulations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:3.5 Communication Strategy with 10 Subpopulations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 compares the four propagation strategies (1to1,
 1toN,
 Nto1 and NtoN) using a fixed configuration of 10 subpopulations.
 Lower numbers of objective function evaluations indicate better optimization performance.
 Overall,
 the NtoN communication strategy consistently achieves the lowest computational effort across the considered benchmark problems,
 whereas the 1to1 strategy generally requires the highest number of objective function evaluations.
 The 1toN and Nto1 strategies provide intermediate performance.
 The cumulative results further confirm the superiority of the NtoN strategy,
 reducing the total number of objective function evaluations from 982,812 for the 1to1 strategy to 297,299,
 corresponding to reductions of approximately 70%,
 33% and 43% compared with the 1to1,
 1toN,
 and Nto1 strategies,
 respectively.
 These findings indicate that simultaneous information exchange among all subpopulations substantially improves cooperation and accelerates convergence,
 making the NtoN communication strategy the most effective communication scheme under the examined experimental configuration.
\end_layout

