\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:tab8-2-2-2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 summarizes the average number of objective function evaluations obtained using four factor formulations.
 The proposed square-root schedule required fewer objective function evaluations than the constant formulation 
\begin_inset Formula $A=1$
\end_inset

 across all examined dimensions.
 Averaged over the eleven benchmark instances,
 the square-root schedule required approximately 5,405 evaluations,
 compared with 5,750 for the constant formulation,
 corresponding to a reduction of approximately 6.0%.
 The largest improvement over 
\begin_inset Formula $A=1$
\end_inset

 was observed for the 150-dimensional instance,
 where the number of evaluations decreased from 6,513 to 4,715 (approximately 27.6%).
 However,
 the exponential and sigmoid schedules achieved lower evaluation counts than the square-root formulation in several dimensions.
 Therefore,
 no single adaptive schedule was uniformly superior across all examined instances.
 Nevertheless,
 the results demonstrate that adaptive formulations generally provide advantages over the constant setting,
 although their relative effectiveness depends on the problem dimension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Performance Comparison with Existing Optimization Algorithms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:The proposed method in comparison with others"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents a comparative evaluation of the proposed optimization algorithm against a set of well-established metaheuristic algorithms,
 including GA 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Genetic,Genetic-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 DE 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "DE,DE1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 GWO 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "GWO,GWO1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 ParallelDE 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "PARALLELDE-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 SAOP 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "SAOP"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 BGWO 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "BGWO1,BGWO2"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and WOA 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "woa,Woa1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 based on the average number of objective function evaluations required for convergence.
 Lower values indicate higher computational efficiency.
 Overall,
 the proposed method demonstrates competitive performance across the considered benchmark suite while maintaining a relatively low computational cost over different problem dimensions.
 Across all benchmark instances,
 the proposed method requires a total of 297,299 objective function evaluations,
 compared with 777,899,
 762,504,
 772,866,
 314,144,
 263,370,
 724,617 and 371,962 for GA,
 DE,
 GWO,
 ParallelDE,
 SAOP,
 BGWO and WOA respectively.
 Although SAOP achieves a lower cumulative number of objective function evaluations,
 the proposed method consistently exhibits competitive performance across the benchmark suite and maintains low computational cost for the majority of benchmark instances.
 These results indicate that the proposed cooperative framework achieves an effective balance between convergence speed and computational efficiency under the considered experimental conditions.
\end_layout

