\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:The-proposed-method"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 compares the examined optimization algorithms in terms of the number of objective function evaluations required to achieve convergence.
 Lower values indicate better computational efficiency.
 The proposed method exhibits one of the lowest median numbers of objective function evaluations while maintaining a relatively small variability across the benchmark problems.
 A Kruskal–Wallis test (p < 0.0001) indicates statistically significant differences among the compared algorithms.
 Overall,
 the results suggest that the proposed method provides competitive optimization performance and a consistent convergence behavior under the considered experimental conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
High-Dimensional Benchmark Problems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To further investigate the scalability of the proposed framework,
 additional experiments were conducted on two high-dimensional benchmark functions with dimensions ranging from 50 to 500.
 Tables 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:table8"
plural "false"
