\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:tab8-2-2-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reports the average wall-clock execution time of the proposed PEAO framework for different numbers of subpopulations.
 Overall,
 the configurations with 10 and 20 subpopulations consistently achieved the lowest execution times across the examined benchmark problems,
 whereas the configurations with 5 and 30 subpopulations generally require longer execution times.
 These results indicate that a moderate number of subpopulations provides a more effective balance between workload distribution and coordination overhead.
 Although the relative differences depend on the benchmark function and problem dimensionality,
 the 10-subpopulation configuration consistently provides competitive execution times across the considered experiments.
 Overall,
 the results suggest that increasing the number of subpopulations beyond a moderate level does not necessarily improve runtime performance under the examined experimental conditions.
