\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Discussion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Effect of the Cooperative Cluster Structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experimental results indicate that the behavior of PEAO is influenced by the number of cooperating subpopulations.
 Under the examined benchmark functions and parameter settings,
 configurations with 10 and 20 subpopulations generally required fewer objective function evaluations than configurations with 5 or 30 subpopulations.
 These findings suggest that a moderate population decomposition provides an effective balance between distributed exploration and local refinement.
 However,
 this observation should not be interpreted as evidence that 10 or 20 subpopulations are universally optimal,
 since the most appropriate configuration may depend on the optimization problem,
 dimensionality,
 population size and computing platform.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Impact of the Propagation Mechanism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The comparison between the configurations with and without propagation showed that enabling propagation consistently reduced the number of objective function evaluations.
 The paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test confirmed that this difference was statistically significant (p < 0.0001).
 These findings suggest that exchanging promising solutions among subpopulations improves search efficiency by reducing redundant exploration and accelerating convergence.
 Since objective function evaluations do not directly measure communication overhead,
 these results should be interpreted together with the wall-clock execution time analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Analysis of Communication Strategies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The comparison of the four communication strategies indicates that the information-sharing topology has a measurable influence on optimization performance.
 Under the examined configuration,
 the N-to-N strategy generally achieved the lowest objective function evaluation counts and the smallest variability.
 The Friedman test indicated statistically significant differences among the evaluated communication schemes.
 Nevertheless,
 the observed advantage of the N-to-N topology is limited to the benchmark functions,
 population size and number of subpopulations considered in this study.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Performance on Practical Optimization Problems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
T
\series default
he experiments on the practical optimization problems provide additional evidence regarding the behavior of the evaluated methods under realistic optimization settings.
 Although the problems differ substantially from the synthetic benchmark functions,
 the proposed method maintained competitive performance in terms of objective function evaluations and solution quality.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Comparative Performance Evaluation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The comparative study indicates that PEAO achieved competitive performance across the examined benchmark suite.
 It obtained relatively low objective function evaluation counts on many problems while maintaining stable performance across different dimensions.
 The Kruskal–Wallis test confirmed statistically significant differences among the compared algorithms,
 indicating that their performance distributions are not identical.
 However,
 these statistical results should be interpreted together with the function-by-function results,
 since no single algorithm consistently achieved the best performance on every benchmark.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Overall Assessment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Overall,
 the experimental results indicate that the proposed PEAO framework provides competitive optimization performance under the examined benchmark functions and experimental settings.
 The sensitivity analyses further suggest that population decomposition,
 propagation,
 communication strategy and adaptive search control all influence optimization efficiency.
 Although several competing algorithms achieved superior performance on individual problems,
 the proposed framework maintained a competitive and consistent performance profile across a broad range of optimization scenarios.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This study presented the Parallel Enzyme Action Optimizer (PEAO),
 a cooperative population-based optimization framework that extends the original EAO through parallel subpopulation evolution,
 controlled propagation,
 alternative communication topologies,
 adaptive search control,
 optional local refinement and a convergence-based stopping criterion.
 The proposed framework was designed to maintain population diversity,
 facilitate information exchange among subpopulations and balance exploration and exploitation throughout the optimization process.
 The experimental analysis demonstrated that several design components substantially influence the behavior of the proposed framework.
 In particular,
 moderate numbers of subpopulations generally require fewer objective function evaluations than smaller or larger decompositions,
 while enabling propagation consistently improved evaluation efficiency.
 Among the examined communication topologies,
 the N-to-N configuration generally required the fewest objective function evaluations when 10 subpopulations were employed.
 The adaptive square-root schedule also provided stable and competitive performance across the examined problem dimensions.
 The comparative experiments indicate that PEAO achieved competitive performance across the considered benchmark problems,
 including high-dimensional instances with up to 500 decision variables.
 The proposed framework generally required comparatively few objective function evaluations while maintaining competitive final-solution quality across a wide range of benchmark functions.
 Nevertheless,
 several competing algorithms achieved superior performance on individual functions or dimensional configurations,
 indicating that no single optimization algorithm consistently outperforms all alternatives.
 The experiments on the practical optimization problems further demonstrate that the proposed framework maintains competitive performance beyond synthetic benchmark functions.
 However,
 since these problems were evaluated only in their original fixed-dimensional formulations,
 they should not be interpreted as evidence of scalability but rather as an assessment of practical optimization performance under the examined conditions.
 Overall,
 the experimental findings suggest that the proposed cooperative framework provides a competitive balance between search efficiency,
 solution quality and robustness under the examined experimental settings.
 The conclusions are limited to the benchmark suite,
 parameter settings,
 stopping criterion,
 implementation and computing platform considered in this study.
 Future work will investigate self-adaptive communication and propagation policies,
 dynamic management of subpopulations,
 derivative-free alternatives to the optional local refinement stage and a more comprehensive parallel scalability analysis using different numbers of processing threads.
 Additional validation on recent benchmark suites and a
\series bold
 
\series default
broader collection of large-scale and real-world optimization problems will also be considered to further assess the generality of the proposed framework.
\end_layout

